again, the data are above the DSSV prediction. The persistence of this trend across numerous observables and multiple detectors convinced DSSV to recalculate their polarized parton distributions. The result is shown in magenta on Figure 5.4: the new DSSV is above the old DSSV at lower \(p_{T}\) values before coming back to join it at the higher values. The previous DSSV polarized gluon distributions corresponded to a value of \(\Delta G=-.1\) (compared to \(\Delta G=1.8\) for GRSV-MAX, \(\Delta G=.4\) for GRSV-STD, and \(\Delta G=.1\) for GRSV-ZERO). The new DSSV polarized gluon distribution corresponds to a value of \(\Delta G=.1\) integrated from \(.05<x<.2\) (over the full x range, the value is \(\Delta G\sim.12\)) [4]. This new value of \(\Delta G\) is almost as large as the quark spin contribution \(\frac{1}{2}\Delta\Sigma\sim.15\). Rather than \(\Delta G\) being negligible, as many people thought, recent \(A_{LL}\) results, including this one, suggest that the gluon spin contribution to the proton spin is actually of a similar magnitude to the quark spin contribution.

The next step from here is to move on to correlated results, such as measuring \(A_{LL}\) for dijets rather than for single-inclusive jets. Figure 5.5 shows the preliminary 2009 STAR dijet \(A_{LL}\) measurement, compared to several theory curves [3]. Dijet and other correlation measurements allow us to start investigating the shape of the \(\Delta g(x,Q^{2})\) distribution, instead of just its first moment. Similarly to the \(\pi^{0}\) and inclusive jet results, the dijet result shows an asymmetry that is larger than predicted by DSSV at lower invariant mass values. If the above result of the non-zero value of \(\Delta G\) holds up under further investigations such as these, it will establish that the polarization of the quarks and gluons account for only about half of the proton spin. To account for the entire proton spin, a non-zero net orbital motion of the quarks and gluons is needed.

